00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.190 align:start position:0%
Chinese police have arrested 12 people  in connection with the series of massive  explosions which killed a hundred and  forty-five people in the city of Tianjin  they include a chairman vice chairman in  three deputy general managers of the  company that had been storing the  chemicals which blew up in August the 12  Sky News has been speaking to ban shoot  ton jack a senior lawyer and chemists at  the center for international  environmental law in Washington we know  that there were there were 700 tons of  sodium cyanide on the facility we also  know that there were numerous other  hazardous substances which were stored  there we know that various organic  substances have been detected following  the explosions these substances are  quite hazardous and in and of themselves  some of them are known neurotoxin some  of them are suspected of having  reproductive abnormalities particularly  when pregnant women are exposed to them  but there's still a great deal that we  don't know we still haven't heard from  the Chinese government exactly what  happened exactly how how much of various  substances that are suspected of being  stored there including ammonium and  potassium nitrates were in fact stored  and how those chemicals came to to be  detonated it's suspected that some  firefighters might have actually  triggered the explosions themselves when  they sprayed water on chemicals that  shouldn't have been extinguished using  water they should have been extinguished  using dry fire extinguishers triggering  what's suspected to be the larger  explosion the second explosion what to  me is truly tragic is that the  government did not have information on  hand either because it wasn't available  or it wasn't accessible that could have  mitigated or even prevented this  disaster it still is not clear the  government still has not provided  information about what it knew at the  time that disaster happened and how that  information flowed to first responders  and communities that were at risk from  from a human rights perspective that is  that is essential information to respect  and protect human rights
 

